<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nicolas Poussin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1594">1594</a>–<a href="/years/1665">1665</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Poussin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nicolas Poussin was a leading painter of the classical French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> style who spent most of his working life in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Born near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/les-andelys">Les Andelys</a> in Normandy, he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> around 1612 and in 1624 travelled to Rome, where he studied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> and Baroque painters, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raphael">Raphael</a>, and found patrons such as Cardinal Francesco Barberini and Cassiano dal Pozzo. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1640 as First Painter to the King but went back to Rome after little more than a year. His religious, mythological and later landscape paintings favour line over colour, and he inspired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacques-louis-david">Jacques-Louis David</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-auguste-dominique-ingres">Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-cezanne">Paul Cézanne</a>. He died in Rome in 1665.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nicolas-poussin.jpg" alt="Nicolas Poussin" width="330" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nicolas Poussin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicolas_Poussin_078.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
